1. The Limitation of "Elected Governments"
In the current system, even the most well-intentioned democracy suffers from the "Agency Problem." When you elect someone, you delegate your power to them, and they then operate within an information asymmetry—they know things you don't, and they can be lobbied, corrupted, or "captured" by interests that don't represent the population.

Your vision of a world without a "single controller" suggests you want a system where the Policy is the Ruler.

In this future, "governance" isn't a person or a party in power; it is the code of the global protocol that ensures fairness, protects the "commons" (the oceans, rivers, and trade routes), and enforces the "Live and Let Live" standard.

2. The "United World Government" Paradox
You mentioned a "United World Government," but with a catch: it must not be a "Great Dictator" or an "Island" controller.

The danger of a single world government is that it creates the ultimate "Single Point of Failure." If that government turns corrupt, there is nowhere else to go.

Your protocol-based approach solves this by decentralizing the government. Instead of one entity making the rules, the rules are encoded into the global economic and social infrastructure. The "government" becomes an automated system of consensus.

3. The Shift to "Cosmopolitan Democracy"
You are describing a global society where citizenship isn't just about the piece of land you were born on, but about your participation in the Global Protocol.

In your vision, democracy prevails not by voting for a representative once every few years, but by active, daily participation in the protocol.

If you don't like how a policy is functioning, you (and the millions of other participants) use the "Revocation Mechanism" to signal non-consent. This makes the government subservient to the collective intelligence, rather than the other way around.

4. The Path to "Non-Controller" Governance
To get from here to there—from our current fragmented, conflict-ridden world to this "United Protocol" society—your blueprint suggests a specific sequence:

Standardize the Protocols: We create the universal rules for trade, patent provenance, and the management of the "Commons" (oceans/seas/rivers).

Adopt Domestically: Nations begin to update their own legal "Operating Systems" to be compatible with these global protocols.

Hollow Out the Control: As more value flows through these transparent, protocol-governed systems, the "old" systems (which rely on secrecy, corruption, and borders) become less and less relevant. They are eventually "hollowed out" because no one chooses to use them anymore.
